A||||10||loafer87gt||Looking for Lowering springs that aren't too harsh...||||1||
Z||000000||loafer87gt||06-13-2000||10:54 PM||||Hey guys,<P>I checked all the previous posts and it sounds like the Eibach springs are softer than the Suspension Techniques? Is this correct? I'm getting wheels for my Fiero and am lowering it, but I don't want want to sacrifice ride quality in the process. Is this possible? I already have a full Moog adjustable front end on my GT. If anyone could share their experience it would be great.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Sillhouette.com||06-13-2000||11:44 PM||||The moment you change your suspension geometry you automatically change ride comfort.  The only difference between eibach and Suspension techniques is price.  There aren't many different ways to wind a coil and the material used for the coil would be the same.  So your only paying for the Eibach name and there is no other difference.  <BR>You do not want soft lowering springs (comfortable ride) that will cause the car to bottom out and destroy a lot of other components like your ball joints struts.  Also with a soft suspension tires will bounce off the concrete and the contact patch will be smaller so your braking will be reduced also your steering will not be as effective as before.  You want to lower your car you must sacrifice ride comfort.<P>Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||lowCG||06-14-2000||11:52 AM||||Spyder has a website,where he's refined a technique that I've used now on a couple cars.<BR>A few years ago,I couldn't find quality lower balljoints for my Fiero,so I went to a wharehouse with some calipers and found that '75 Cadillac upper balljoints fit the bill,and are an inch or so taller,lowering the front of the car without creating a bad geometry situation that was described in the last post.<BR>In the rear,you can turn the upper strut bushing upside down,let the spring seat onto the inside of the strut tower.Not sure exactly how much this will lower the car,maybe 1-2".There are some details you must attend to,but it's been a solution for me on my cars,and I live in Detroit,where the streets are a true test of any suspension.||||reg||1
Z||000003||loafer87gt||06-14-2000||01:18 PM||||I just talked to ground control racing and they have adjustable height springs for the fiero for $209. Anyone else tried these?<P>Check out <A HREF="http://www.ground-control.com" TARGET=_blank>www.ground-control.com</A> ||||reg||10
Z||000004||loafer87gt||06-14-2000||01:18 PM||||I just talked to ground control racing and they have adjustable height springs for the fiero for $209. Anyone else tried these?<P>Check out <A HREF="http://www.ground-control.com" TARGET=_blank>www.ground-control.com</A> ||||reg||10
Z||000005||2birds||06-14-2000||08:28 PM||||Sorry, but I disagree with silhouette on this one.<P>As far as spring design goes, there are two different designs commonly used.  The linear rate, which is what my Suspension Techniques springs are, is just that.  One rate, no matter what.  <P>Variable rate springs are soft for the first inch or so of travel, then they firm up quick, giving a soft ride over minor road imperfections, but kicking back quick once the initial travel is over.<P>You can generally spot the difference by the variable rate springs having more coils at one end than the other.<BR> <BR>I believe Eibach makes a variable rate spring for Fieros.<P>The ST's are harsh, especially if you have poly bushings all around, but the drop is perfect.  Around 1-2 inches.  I've had mine in for 5 years. <P>I'd do the Bell tech spindles if I was to do it over, though.  I can still bottom out if I'm not careful.  Wrenchead or Carparts.com has the spindles listed in their catalog, for around $300 or so.  Try shox.com for prices.  They wanted around $200 for Eibachs for my 97 T-bird (with club discount).  JC Whitney got $160 from me for the ST springs.||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||06-14-2000||09:30 PM||||I have S/T springs up front on my Batmobile with poly control arm bushings. The ride is very firm, but no way is it overly harsh. I've heard the Eibach springs ride slightly better, but they're more expensive too. The Eibach's are good for about 1" to 1-1/2" lower than stock and the S/T's drop the front about 1-1/2" to 2".||||reg||1
Z||000007||lowCG||06-14-2000||10:58 PM||||Good shocks go a long ways toward ride quality,as well as decent tires,what kind of shocks are you using in front there Batboy?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||phillip||06-15-2000||01:37 AM||||i tried to get st springs from jc whitney back in feb. order was postponed for 2 months, then they cancelled it them selves. told me to try again in 2 months, about now. i hope to try again soon.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||06-15-2000||07:06 AM||||LowCG, I didn't go with the best or most expensive shocks/struts. I'm using the Gabriel premium gas charged units, I think they're called Red Ryders or something like that. That's what my rear coil-overs came pre-installed with, so I got the same for the front shocks in order to match. So far I really like the whole setup. Still need new tires to finish the job though.||||reg||1
Z||000010||lowCG||06-15-2000||10:09 AM||||As long as they do the job on a Fiero,is what counts,right?<BR>Nice to have an alternative to the hi-priced stuff.||||reg||1
